## 4. Registering the module (two code spots + one sheet row)
 
A handler name must appear in **BOTH** of these in `Code.gs`, and ONLY when the
handler's `.gs` file is actually in the project (otherwise "X is not defined"):
 
1. `getModuleHandler()` — maps the handler name string to the code object.
2. `getRegisteredHandlers()` — lists the handler name for the Module Manager.
 
Then add the **Modules sheet row** via Admin → Modules (Key, Label, Icon,
Roles, Handler, Include, Order, Enabled). Deploying code does **not** create the
row — registration is a separate, deliberate step. Keep a module's registration
commented out in `Code.gs` until its `.gs` file ships.
 
---
 
## 5. Using platform services
 
### Tasks — the "needs attention" queue (reporting only)
 
Create a task when work needs a human; resolve it when the underlying record
reaches a terminal state. A task is a **pointer**: it stores routing + a
back-reference, never business data. The module's own sheet is the source of
truth.
 
```js
// Create — assignedTo is a specific user (normal case);
// dueAt / staleAfterDays are optional and DOMAIN-supplied.
const { taskId } = Tasks.create({
  module: 'thesis',
  sourceType: 'thesis_submission',
  sourceId: recordId,                 // id in YOUR module's sheet
  label: 'Thesis awaiting sponsor review',
  assignedTo: sponsorEmail,
  dueAt: new Date('2026-04-15'),      // optional
  staleAfterDays: 14,                 // optional
});
 
// Resolve when your record is done with this step:
Tasks.resolve(taskId, { resolvedBy: user });
// or, without having stored the id:
Tasks.resolveForSource('thesis', recordId, { resolvedBy: user });
```
 
**Tasks reports and escalates visibility; it never owns workflow.** Advancing,
returning, recalling, or reassigning a process is workflow logic and lives in
the module (or a future Workflow layer) — never in Tasks. When the workflow
moves a record, the module calls `Tasks.resolve` on the old task and
`Tasks.create` for the next one.
 
### Notify — email delivery (content stays in your module)
 
Notify resolves recipients, dedupes, formats consistently, and log-and-continues
so a mail failure never breaks your action. It does NOT know what your message
says — you supply the wording.
 
```js
const to = Notify.resolveRecipients({
  superAdmins: CONFIG.SUPER_ADMINS,
  roleRules: NotifyRules.recipientsFor(someRole),
  explicit: [sponsorEmail],
});
Notify.send({ to, subject: 'Thesis submitted', body: '...your wording...' });
```
 
Do not call `Utils.sendEmail` directly for module notifications; route through
Notify so formatting/dedup/logging stay consistent.
 
### EventBus — react to platform events without coupling
 
If your module should react to something another module does, register a
listener in `getEventListeners()` in `Code.gs` (append-only) — never edit the
emitting module. Emit your own events so future modules can react to you:
 
```js
EventBus.emit('thesis.submitted', { recordId, sponsorEmail }, { user });
```
 
A listener that throws is logged/audited and skipped; it can't break the
emitter.
 
### Auth — identity (read, don't duplicate)
 
Get names/IDs/roles via `Auth.getProfile(email)` / `Auth.getRoles(email)`.
Never store your own copy of a person's name or ID. Email is the primary
identity key; IDs are a backup match path (see PersonMatch).
 
### DataService — sheet CRUD by header name
 
`insert`, `update`, `query`, `getAll`, `generateId`. It fills
`CreatedAt/CreatedBy/UpdatedAt/UpdatedBy` automatically when those columns
exist. Always include those meta columns in your tab's headers.
 
### TestMode — end-to-end workflow tests without bothering real people
 
An authorized functional account can walk your whole workflow while every
assignment and email routes to mapped test accounts. Dispatch owns
activation (a `_test:true` payload from a rostered account); your module
opts in with a few conventions:
 
1. **Declare a `TEST_SLOTS` manifest** on your handler — the workflow
   POSITIONS a test routes through, not roles or people:
   `[{ key: 'sponsor', label: 'Faculty Sponsor', defaultRole: SPONSOR_ROLE }]`.
   Export it like `TABS`/`CHANNELS` (`TEST_SLOTS: TEST_SLOTS` — not an
   action). `defaultRole` is both the Admin-UI hint for the role the
   account genuinely needs and the global-default lookup key. Admin →
   Testing assigns which account fills each slot.
2. **Add `TestMode` and `TestSelections` columns** to your record tab's
   `SETUP_SCHEMA` headers (before the meta columns), and run
   `addMissingColumns()` for an existing tab. `TestMode` flags the record;
   `TestSelections` preserves what the tester REALLY selected.
3. **Substitute at WRITE time, after real validation.** In your submit
   action, run the real eligibility/PersonMatch checks on the tester's
   real selection first — that validation is half of what a test
   exercises — then:
   `TestMode.assertReady(MODULE);` (refuse if any slot is unmapped —
   fail closed) and
   `const finalX = TestMode.substitute(MODULE, 'slotKey', selectedX);`
   Write `finalX` on the record, plus
   `TestMode: TestMode.recordFlag()` and
   `TestSelections: TestMode.selectionNote({ slotKey: selectedX })` when
   the substitution changed anything — the canonical `slot=value` form,
   never hand-rolled prose, so `TestMode.selectedFor(rec, 'slotKey')` can
   recover the real pick later. Use `finalX` in duplicate keys, routing,
   and event emits. Then GUARD the write: after inserting, re-read the
   record and throw (removing a fresh insert) if the execution is a test
   but the stored flag isn't TRUE — a silently dropped stamp (missing
   columns) otherwise resurfaces three stages later as a half-real record
   mailing real people. See `_assertTestStamp` in IndividualStudiesModule.
   Outside a test, every call is a no-op.
4. **Re-enter test mode at later stages.** Call
   `TestMode.activateForRecord(rec, user)` right after loading a record in
   every action that routes or notifies — the flag traveled with the
   record, so later stages need no client cooperation.
5. **Intercept role-pool hops.** Where a task is created with
   `assignedRole` (a shared staff pool), route to the slot account during
   a test instead:
   `const t = TestMode.active() ? TestMode.substitute(MODULE, 'slot', '') : '';`
   then `assignedTo: t` when set, else the role — and email only `t`. A
   test task must never land on real staff dashboards. (One-off
   notification targets with no slot of their own can lean on the Notify
   backstop, which redirects ALL mail to the tester during a test — but
   the backstop covers mail only, never tasks.)
6. **Offer the UI checkbox via `testModeAvailable`.** A tiny action
   returning `{ available: TestMode.isTestAccount(user, roles) && TestMode.readiness(MODULE).ready }`;
   the client shows the checkbox only when true and sends `_test: true` on
   submit. Expose the record's flag in your public record
   (`testMode: _isTrueStr(r.TestMode)`) and badge it TEST in lists.
7. **Never put test conditionals in permission checks.** Substitution
   means the record honestly names the functional account, so ownership
   checks, queues, and per-action gates all work unmodified. If you find
   yourself writing `if (TestMode.active())` inside an authorization
   check, the design is wrong. The line to hold is *authorization vs
   fidelity*: a SIMULATION-FIDELITY conditional — e.g. running a schedule
   lookup against `TestMode.selectedFor(rec, 'slot')` because the
   substituted functional account has no sections of its own — is fine
   and encouraged, because it makes the test exercise MORE real behavior.
   What must stay test-blind is anything that grants, denies, or routes
   authority.
 
